ur valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \n- You hold **Jack of Clubs, Jack of Diamonds**, and the board is **Queen of Hearts, 4 of Hearts, 2 of Clubs, King of Clubs, King of Hearts**.  \n- The best 5-card hand you can make is **Kings and Jacks (Two Pair)**: K-K-J-J-Q. This is a strong two pair, but not the absolute nuts.\n- However, note that any player holding an Ace completes a **Broadway straight (A-K-Q-J-10)** — which would beat your two pair. But there’s no 10 on the board, so unless someone has A-10 or J-10, that’s unlikely.\n- More importantly, **the board pairs both Kings and there\'s a possible flush draw with three hearts (Q♥, 4♥, K♥)**. If an opponent holds two hearts (e.g., A♥ or J♥), they could have a **flush**.\n- But since you\'re at the **river** (final betting round), all cards are out. Your two pair (Kings and Jacks) is fairly strong unless someone has:\n  - King-Jack (full house),\n  - Pocket Kings or Queens (which would give them trips or better),\n  - Or two hearts making a flush.\n\nBut consider:  \n- You raised aggressively pre-flop (+200), then again post-flop (+420), and again on the turn (+100). This signals strength, possibly representing AK, KK, QQ, or AQ.\n- Your opponent has **called every bet so far**, including raises, which suggests they have *some* kind of strong hand — possibly top pair or better.\n- Given the board, possible hands for your opponent include:\n  - **King-Queen (two pair, K-Q)** — worse than your K-J-J.\n  - **King-King or Queen-Queen (trips)** — beats you.\n  - **King-4 or 4-2 (two pair, K-4 or 4-2)** — worse than yours.\n  - **Flush (with any two hearts)** — beats you.\n  - **Bluff or weaker kings** — possible, but less likely given calling pattern.\n\nYour current hand (J-J) gives you **second-best two pair** (Kings full of Jacks isn\'t possible — only one Jack on board), wait: correction — actually:\n\nWait: **Board: Q♥, 4♥, 2♣, K♣, K♥**  \nYou: J♣, J♦\n\nSo community cards contain: K♣, K♥ → two Kings  \nYou add: J♣, J♦ → two Jacks  \nSo your best five-card hand is: **K-K-J-J-Q** → Two Pair, Kings and Jacks, Queen kicker.\n\nNo full house. No three Jacks. Only two Jacks in hand.\n\nThus: **Two Pair: Kings and Jacks** — very strong, but vulnerable to:\n- Any **King-Jack** (would make full house: K-K-K-J-J)\n- Any **K-K or K-Q** (could be stronger two pair or trips)\n- Any **hearts flush**: Need three hearts in hand? No — board has **three hearts**: Q♥, 4♥, K♥ → so **any player with two hearts makes a flush**.\n\nSo if opponent has A♥X♥ or even 10♥5♥, they have a flush.\n\nAlso, **straight draws failed** — no 10 or J on board except your Jacks, so Broadway straight requires A-10.\n\nBut back to action: It\'s the **river**, and your opponent just **checked** (action: 0).\n\nThat means: **You are first to act on the river**, after previous betting rounds.\n\nWait — reviewing history:\n> On Round 3, your opponent performed action: 0.\n\nRound 3 = River. Opponent checked.\n\nNow it\'s your turn.\n\nYou now have the option to:\n- Check behind (0): end the hand, go to showdown.\n- Bet: raise by at least 100 (since min-bet is 100 more than previous bet? Wait — need to clarify bet sizing).\n\nWait — valid actions: [-2, -1, 0, 100, or any value >100 up to chips]\n\nThis suggests that **minimum bet/raise is 100**, probably because last bet was 100 (you raised 100 on the turn), and no raise since.\n\nBut opponent **checked** on river — so no new bet.\n\nTherefore: **There is no outstanding bet.** So you can check (0), or make a **value bet or bluff bet**.\n\nMinimum bet size is typically the big blind or defined increment — here, options include "100" as a choice, suggesting **min-bet is 100**.\n\nSo your choices: fold (-1), all-in (-2), check (0), bet 100, or bet more.\n\nNow, evaluating:\n\nOption 1: **Check (0)**  \n- Lets showdown happen. If