s likely beats you**.\n\nWhat about pure bluff or weak hands? Like 10-9? They have no pair — high card K, Q — worse than your two pair.\n\nBut such hands would likely have folded earlier, especially after facing multiple raises.\n\nGiven that opponent called all your bets:\n- Pre-flop: you raised 200, they called.\n- Flop: they raised 210, you raised to 420, they called.\n- Turn: you bet 100, they called.\n- River: they checked.\n\nTheir line suggests:\n- Possibly started with K-x, Q-x, or drawing hand.\n- Called flop raise — maybe had top pair or draw.\n- Now checks river — maybe missed flush draw or has medium strength.\n\nBut crucially: **if they have any King, they have three of a kind, which beats your two pair**.\n\nSo when do you win?\n\nOnly if they have:\n- No King, no Queen, no pair, and not two hearts.\n\nFor example:\n- 10-9 offsuit: hand is K-K-Q-10-9 → one pair Kings → loses to your two pair.\n- But such a hand would likely have folded earlier.\n\nAlternatively, they could have **bluffed the flop** with air or flush draw that missed.\n\nBut after calling three streets, it\'s unlikely they have pure air.\n\nMost plausible: they have a **King**, giving them three of a kind — which beats you.\n\nOr they have **Queen**, giving them two pair (Kings and Queens) — which beats your Kings and Jacks.\n\nOr they have **two hearts** — flush.\n\nSo **your hand is likely behind**.\n\nTherefore, **checking back (0)** is safer — avoid putting more chips in when behind.\n\nBetting 100 would only:\n- Get called by hands that beat you (flush, full house, trips, two pair with Q),\n- Fold out hands you beat (like one pair or worse two pair).\n\nSo **no value** — only downside.\n\nHence, best action: **Check (0)**, see showdown.\n\nYou’ve already invested heavily. Don’t bluff or value bet a losing hand.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: My two pair (Kings and Jacks) is likely beaten by opponent’s range, given their calling pattern and the board pairing Kings and having three hearts. Most hands they play to this point either have a King (making trips), Queen (making better two pair), or a flush. Betting would only get calls from better hands.\n\nReason: Avoid further losses. Checking allows me to control pot size and proceed to showdown without risking more chips when likely behind.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
