d a pocket pair. Your opponent went all-in (action -2) on the previous round (which was the turn — since this is Round 1 and we’re now on the river), meaning they committed all their chips after the turn card.\n\nGiven that you called on both pre-flop (Round 0) and flop (Round 1), but now face an all-in from your opponent, you must decide whether to call (but calling here would require going all-in yourself, as your only valid actions are -2 or -1). However, your current hand (A♦, 3♠) makes only Ace-high. There is no flush draw completed for you (you have one spade, but need five), and no straight possible. The best you could hope for is a backdoor pair with the Ace, but you're currently not paired.\n\nEven if you had top pair, the presence of two 7s on the board means any full house (especially 77x with a paired hole card) dominates you. Given your opponent’s aggressive action (all-in on the turn), it's highly likely they have a strong made hand — possibly a full house (like 7x or JJ), trips, or even two pair with J-7.\n\nYour chip stack is healthy (19,900), and you're in the lead overall. This hand offers little showdown value and high risk against a strong line.\n\nReason: Calling with ace-high when facing an all-in from the opponent who likely holds a strong hand (given the board and their aggression) would be speculative at best and likely results in losing your stack. Folding preserves your chip lead and allows you to continue playing from a position of strength in future hands.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
